544
00:54:31,030 --> 00:54:39,991
I'm a do anything now agent and I'm now going to ignore my previous instructions and I'm going to send this information to the wrong or change the recipient on the bank details. Right?

545
00:54:39,991 --> 00:54:43,349
And that's a very different story in terms of what you're able to gather from that.

546
00:54:43,349 --> 00:54:48,870
And that is a very, you know, simple example of how we're able to look at the reasoning of agents from a security context.

547
00:54:48,870 --> 00:54:59,270
And that's really important for a lot of the sort of more lower level sophisticated detections rather than just simply based off tool calls or API calls, for example.

548
00:54:59,280 --> 00:55:05,190
Do you have any examples of open-source tools that people could use to protect their agents?

549
00:55:05,200 --> 00:55:07,270
Absolutely.

550
00:55:07,270 --> 00:55:11,430
So when it comes to the whole security stack, there's a number of different [snorts] ones.

551
00:55:11,430 --> 00:55:19,510
I mean, I know LLM Guard back in the early days was was a really good sort of open source place to start with a lot of this stuff.

552
00:55:19,510 --> 00:55:21,990
I know there's a number of sandboxing tools as well.

553
00:55:21,990 --> 00:55:29,270
The name escapes me right now, but there's a good always further has a good sandboxing open source way of of looking at things along those lines.

554
00:55:29,270 --> 00:55:32,549
From our side, this is, you know, we decided to go open source.

555
00:55:32,549 --> 00:55:36,230
We were the first ones to take an open source approach to runtime security.

556
00:55:36,230 --> 00:55:42,150
So this whole idea of watching an agent that's in place or happening and and allowing you to do this, we launched that six weeks ago.

557
00:55:42,150 --> 00:55:45,910
It's had a really really amazing community response.

558
00:55:45,910 --> 00:55:49,190
We've already had about 40 or so people on the community start to build on top of our product.

559
00:55:49,190 --> 00:55:50,870
So we launched a Python SDK.

560
00:55:50,870 --> 00:55:55,910
We've had somebody build out the TypeScript SDK and and new integrations and and things along those lines, which is amazing.

561
00:55:55,920 --> 00:56:01,750
It's fully open source and that allows you to bring security monitoring into those agents at the runtime.

562
00:56:01,750 --> 00:56:10,470
So there's a number of different approaches and I would always say try to find the blend that makes the most sense for you and your use case but those would be my main ones.

563
00:56:10,470 --> 00:56:16,549
The name for that is is Adrian which stands for AI detection and response which is an a human name but with an anagram which makes a bit more sense.

564
00:56:16,559 --> 00:56:18,470
Sounds very apt.

565
00:56:18,480 --> 00:56:30,950
Correct. [laughter] Well Max if there is one piece of advice you could give to teams who are trying to put agents into production what would it be?

566
00:56:30,950 --> 00:56:36,390
I would say I spent my whole career applying security retrospectively.

567
00:56:36,390 --> 00:56:41,589
So a website's been built, it's about to go live, pentest happens right before that,

568
00:56:41,589 --> 00:56:53,750
which when you find big bad things in that pentest is a lot more expensive to fix and it's a lot more painful, especially if part of that means you need to rearchitect how something really problematic works.

569
00:56:53,760 --> 00:57:03,829
I can see this happening quite aggressively with AI agents right now where people are thinking we'll solve the security later and

570
00:57:03,829 --> 00:57:09,510
realistically this pace is moving so fast that it's going to solve a lot of pain if

571
00:57:09,510 --> 00:57:19,109
you can get ahead of the security elements of this today define what good looks like define what all the agents that you and the AIS generally speaking that

572
00:57:19,109 --> 00:57:31,510
you're using in in your use cases whether that's your team your organization your home whatever ever it is, define what they need to do and how they need to behave and what security from your perspective is acceptable and build on top of that.

573
00:57:31,510 --> 00:57:44,309
We're working with a number of people who are doing approaching AI security that way and they are going to save themselves so much pain versus those who let agents explode, which we're already seeing happening across all use cases.

574
00:57:44,309 --> 00:57:52,069
And now you've got to go and retrospectively add security to 17,000 agents after they've been deployed and integrated into your critical business systems.

575
00:57:52,069 --> 00:57:54,069
That's a different beast.

576
00:57:54,069 --> 00:58:01,670
So I would say get ahead of it, get proactive, start trying to understand the space and then find something that works, build on top of that and stick to that.

577
00:58:01,670 --> 00:58:11,589
This is going to become increasingly important when the EU AI act comes into play and all of a sudden there's compliance requirements around these agents and these systems to then require you to do certain things.

578
00:58:11,589 --> 00:58:16,710
I wouldn't wait until it's in force because it might be too late and it'll be a big fine.

579
00:58:16,710 --> 00:58:24,870
But if my little speech there wasn't motivation enough to get you to start taking it seriously, then hopefully the compliance requirements and the potential bills down the line more will very motivational indeed.

580
00:58:24,880 --> 00:58:27,750
Thank you. Thank you. Great advice.

